### 3.4 Optimizing the Adversarial RGB-T Pattern

We develop a gradient-based algorithm to optimize the variables of NORP. Since pip\_{i} is discrete in Equation [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.04675#S3.E2 "Equation 2 ‣ 3.3 Designing Non-Overlapping RGB-T Pattern ‣ 3 Method ‣ Physical Adversarial Clothing Evades Visible-Thermal Detectors via Non-Overlapping RGB-T Pattern"), we optimize a continuous variable pi~\tilde{p\_{i}} instead, and discretize pi~\tilde{p\_{i}} as pi=𝟏​(p~i≥0.5)p\_{i}=\mathbf{1}(\tilde{p}\_{i}\geq 0.5) after the optimization. However, a naive method to optimize the variables [ri(V),gi(V),bi(V),p~i][r^{\mathrm{(V)}}\_{i},g^{\mathrm{(V)}}\_{i},b^{\mathrm{(V)}}\_{i},\tilde{p}\_{i}] directly led to suboptimal results (see Sec. [4.6](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.04675#S4.SS6 "4.6 Ablation Study for SDCO method ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Physical Adversarial Clothing Evades Visible-Thermal Detectors via Non-Overlapping RGB-T Pattern") for details). This is because the continuous variables ri(V)r^{\mathrm{(V)}}\_{i}, gi(V)g^{\mathrm{(V)}}\_{i}, bi(V)b^{\mathrm{(V)}}\_{i} and the discrete variable pip\_{i} are entangled, thus the approximation of pip\_{i} affects other variables as well.

